{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789496914,"nonce":"pJ3r3EPd6yKhNY/q1Y+cuHHP+LE=","message":"Implemented in the working tree; leaving this issue open until the changes land.\n\nThe common signal reproduces with:\n\n    xrange = range\n    for i in xrange(2):\n        print(i)\n\nBefore the change, this exits with SIGSEGV. A bare range name had no function-value lowering, so the alias did not hold a callable record. range now becomes a variadic function record accepting one, two or three integer bounds; the shared call dispatcher packs positional arguments for variadic records. Ordinary fixed-arity records retain their existing call shape. Like the existing range expression path, this materializes the sequence as a list.\n\nA second correctness defect was masked by the signal: dynamic dispatch of a method returning None discarded the method call when coercing its result to a dynamic value. Point.normalize() therefore never ran through a dynamic receiver. Coercion now evaluates the expression before yielding the null result, including coercion to a nullable class pointer.\n\nAdded CPython-pinned conformance cases: inference/range_as_value.py and class/dynamic_none_return.py. They cover alias calls, 1/2/3 bounds, higher-order and list-held callables, rebinding, invalid arity/type/zero-step errors, mutation by None-returning methods, exception propagation, and calls used as list elements.\n\nValidation:\n- cargo test --release -p zyntax_python -p zyntax_builtins: all 38 tests pass, including all 22 conformance categories and memory-pressure checks (existing known conformance failures remain expected).\n- Original, unedited float kernel completes --fast (5 iterations): zypy mean 52.94 ms, PyPy 9.29 ms, CPython 50.54 ms in this local run. This is a correctness fix, not a PyPy performance win.\n- Imported the unedited float kernel and printed benchmark(100000).x/y/z: byte-for-byte equal to CPython.\n- Imported the unedited nbody_modified kernel, called offset_momentum(BODIES[\"sun\"]) and advance(0.01, 20000), then printed all five BODIES entries: byte-for-byte equal to CPython. Also checked 10 steps.\n- The original nbody_modified harness now gets past the signal and raises TypeError: function takes 2 positional arguments but 1 were given. util calls the value main(n, ref=\"sun\") with only n; the function-value adapter does not honour defaults. This is the same record/arity limitation tracked by 27cd9bf; adding the module-function case there.\n\nThe benchmark sources and harness are unchanged. 89aaf85 remains the index and 39c723b remains open for the website chart.","files":null}]}